<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#uruk-period">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uruk period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 4000–3100 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> period is the archaeological period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> from about 4000 to 3100 BCE, named after the city of Uruk. Its later phase saw the growth of the first true cities, monumental temples, the cylinder seal and the earliest proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> writing, and an Uruk expansion of colonies and trading outposts reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uruk-period.jpg" alt="Uruk period" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Zunkir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uruk_expansion.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> period is named after the city of Uruk in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and follows the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a> and precedes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jemdet-nasr-period">Jemdet Nasr period</a>, which is often treated as its final stage. In the broadest sense it covers the whole fourth millennium BCE.</p><p>The term was coined at a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1930, together with the Ubaid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jemdet-nasr-period">Jemdet Nasr periods</a>, using data from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, Ubayd, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>, Jemdet Nasr and Susa and the first discoveries made by German archaeologists at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> in 1929. The Uruk culture is marked by what is sometimes called an urban revolution: the appearance of cities and the state, with a greater specialisation of activities and functions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> was the most productive farmland in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, with irrigation based on barley, date palms and sheep kept for wool, and the marshes supplied fish, birds and reeds. Innovations included irrigated agriculture with the ard, a wooden plough, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potters-wheel">potter's wheel</a>, standardised ceramics and bricks produced in large quantities, and large-scale wool production in textile workshops.</p><p>Instruments for supervising workers and resources developed with state institutions, and the first writing, proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, appears around 3400 to 3300 BCE, essentially for administrative purposes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> itself was the largest settlement by far: it may have covered 230 to 250 hectares in the Late Uruk period, with a population of between 25,000 and 50,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>These innovations were once thought to have begun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, but neighbouring regions, including Susiana, north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, took part in the process. The Uruk expansion saw the setting up of outposts and colonies from southern Mesopotamia on trade routes, at sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godin-tepe">Godin Tepe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> and Hacinebi, and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habuba-kabira">Habuba Kabira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-aruda">Jebel Aruda</a>. Beveled-rim bowls typical of the culture are found from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. The Late Uruk phase, about 3500 to 3300 or 3200 BCE, marked the peak, and after about 3300 or 3200 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> became culturally more fragmented.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uruk period” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Growth was so rapid that archaeologists named a whole era after it: in the <b>Uruk period</b>, c. 4000–3100 BCE, the city became the largest settlement in the world, covering perhaps 250 hectares by the late fourth millennium, with a population often estimated at 20,000 to 40,000 and possibly…</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In southern Mesopotamia, the land later known as Sumer, the period marks the earliest known settlement of the alluvial plain and runs from about 6500 to 3800 BCE, when the <b>Uruk period</b> followed.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ubaid-culture">Ubaid culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Near East chiefdoms gave way after 4000 BCE to the cities and states of the <b>Uruk period</b>, but elsewhere chiefly rule long remained usual.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet there were no real cities until the <b>Uruk period</b>, warfare seems to have been extremely rare, and Ubaid pottery and customs spread from south-eastern Turkey to the Persian Gulf, in Gil Stein and Rana Özbal's account, largely through the peaceful spread of ideas rather than conquest.</blockquote><a href="/essays/before-the-state">Before the State</a></li></ul></section>
